from author_proof_obligations import add
add('P169-0002','criterion with proof following','Proposition 3.6.2: derived functors and filtered colimits','Under AB5 and a generator, the stated commutation and acyclicity hypotheses imply that right derived functors commute with filtered colimits.','Make filteredness of the indexing preorder explicit, construct compatible injective resolutions, and commute cohomology with filtered colimits in the target. Use the additive/left-exact scope needed by the chosen acyclic-resolution theorem; an arbitrary non-left-exact T cannot be silently substituted into that proof.','filtered colimit|derived functor|acyclic')
add('P169-0003','diagram-injective step omitted','Proposition 3.6.2: compatible injective resolutions','An injective resolution in the diagram category is termwise injective, and its filtered colimit computes the required derived comparison.','Repair the generator argument by the genuine free-diagram left adjoints to evaluation. These left adjoints are exact because their values are coproducts and AB5 implies AB4; evaluation therefore preserves injectives. Then apply filtered-colimit exactness in both categories and the termwise acyclicity hypothesis.','diagram injective|evaluation adjoint|filtered colimit')
add('P170-0001','compactness argument left to reader','Proposition 3.6.3: cohomology and filtered colimits of sheaves','The relevant section functor commutes with filtered colimits in the compact/support or noetherian cases stated in the proposition.','Represent germs by sections at finite stages, use compactness or noetherian quasi-compactness to pass to a finite cover, and use filteredness to choose a common stage for all section and overlap data. For support conditions control a closed neighborhood as required; check which separation and support assumptions enter.','filtered colimit|compact support|noetherian space')
add('P170-0002','corollary proof omitted','Corollary to Proposition 3.6.3: cohomological-dimension test','On the stated compact or noetherian spaces, vanishing on all Z_U in degrees above n detects cohomological dimension at most n.','Apply the filtered-colimit result and Proposition 3.6.1 with the zero thick subcategory. Necessity is immediate; sufficiency uses both the local-generator reduction and its compatibility with filtered unions.','cohomological dimension|Z_U|noetherian')
add('P170-0003','base case needs topological qualification','Theorem 3.6.5: zero-dimensional base case','A noetherian space of combinatorial dimension zero has the cohomology of its finite discrete Kolmogorov quotient; the space itself need not be discrete without T0.','Show that there are finitely many minimal closed irreducible components and that their indistinguishability classes form a finite discrete quotient. Sheaves and sections descend across this quotient, giving exact global sections. The two-point indiscrete space disproves the parenthetical claim without T0, while preserving the vanishing theorem.','noetherian|dimension zero|T0|indiscrete')
add('P171-0001','induction supplied with geometric steps suppressed','Theorem 3.6.5: noetherian dimension bound','The cohomology of a noetherian space vanishes above its finite combinatorial dimension.','Use finitely many irreducible components, verify the cokernel is supported on their pairwise intersections of smaller dimension, and reduce to the irreducible case. There use the closed-open sequence and acyclicity of the constant sheaf. The final degree shift applies in the positive degrees needed by the induction; the base case uses the quotient qualification.','Grothendieck vanishing|noetherian dimension|irreducible')
add('P171-0002','example asserted without proof','Theorem 3.6.5, Remark 2: dimension bound need not be sharp','Well-ordered spaces with the corrected initial-segment topology can have cohomological dimension zero and arbitrarily large combinatorial dimension.','Include the empty set and whole space among closed sets, verify arbitrary unions/intersections required for the topology and the descending-chain condition, and note that the least point has only the whole space as a neighborhood. Global sections then equal its exact stalk functor. Exhibit chains of irreducible closed initial segments to measure combinatorial dimension.','well-ordered space|cohomological dimension|combinatorial dimension')
add('P171-0003','sheaf and support verification omitted','§3.7: direct image with relative supports','The local support condition defines a sheaf of sections on the base and a left-exact direct-image functor.','Glue sections by the ordinary sheaf axiom and verify the support condition locally near each base point; restriction must preserve that condition. Distinguish this locally defined relative support family from a fixed global support condition.','direct image with supports|relative support|Leray')
add('P171-0004','formula split across page boundary','§3.7: global sections of the relative direct image','The global section identity on the following page identifies sections of f_Psi(F) with sections supported in Psi(X).','Read this cue together with the complete following formula: it is the definition at the whole base space, not an independent unnamed theorem. Any further identification with another support family requires its explicit local-to-global support condition.','relative direct image|support family')
add('P172-0001','product calculation in supplied proof','Proposition 3.7.1, case 1: ordinary direct image preserves injectives','For ordinary direct image, the Godement product pushes forward to the product indexed by fibres, and injectivity follows.','Apply the ordinary-direct-image hypothesis of case 1 when identifying the whole fibre product; this equality does not hold for unrestricted smaller support families. Each fibre product of injective groups is injective, and an injective sheaf is a direct summand of its Godement product. Alternatively use the exact inverse-image adjunction.','direct image|injective sheaf|Godement product')
add('P173-0001','derived sheafification identification omitted','Proposition 3.7.3: higher direct-image sheaves','The derived relative direct image is the sheafification of the corresponding local supported cohomology presheaf under the stated acyclicity hypotheses.','Resolve injectively on the source, restrict to inverse images of opens, and compare the derived section functors using preservation of the needed acyclic objects. Sheafification is exact, so the cohomology sheaf is the sheafification of the presheaf cohomology.','higher direct image|cohomology presheaf|Leray')
add('P174-0001','definition with refinement details omitted','§3.8: Cech cohomology as a limit over covers','The cover cohomology groups form a directed system independent of the selected refinement maps up to the induced cohomology maps.','Write the standard prism homotopy between two refinement maps, verify signs, and use common refinements to define the directed system. Treat covers as a set-sized cofinal indexing family in the chosen universe. This is a construction check, not an assertion of equality with derived cohomology.','Cech cohomology|refinement homotopy|open cover')
add('P174-0002','contracting homotopy and comparison omitted','§3.8: Cech effaceability and degree-one comparison','Product sheaves are Cech-acyclic for every cover; positive Cech functors are effaceable, and in degrees zero and one they agree with derived cohomology.','For each point choose one covering index containing it and insert it into the cochain indices to obtain a contraction of the pointwise product complex. Then embed into the product sheaf. For higher degrees require a cohomological delta-functor structure; on paracompact spaces prove the needed refinement and lifting result before invoking universality.','Cech effaceability|Godement|paracompact comparison')
add('P174-0003','abstract spectral-sequence step sketched','§3.8: fixed-cover Cartan–Leray construction','The cover cochain functor and degree-zero cohomology give the Cartan–Leray spectral sequence.','Take an injective sheaf resolution, form the cover double complex and identify the two filtrations. The cover cochain complex of an injective sheaf has no positive cohomology, which verifies the composite-functor acyclicity condition.','Cartan Leray|Cech|double complex')
add('P175-0001','calculations within supplied construction','Theorem 3.8.1: fixed-cover E2 page and abutment','The E2 page is cover cohomology with coefficients in the cohomology presheaves, and the abutment is sheaf cohomology of X.','Use exactness of products of abelian groups, preservation of injectives by open restriction, and compatibility of the restriction maps with the Cech differential. Establish that the identifications commute with refinement and with the edge morphism. Correct the left-exactness and restriction-order slips separately in the text.','Cartan Leray|cohomology presheaf|edge map')
add('P176-0001','local-vanishing proof supplied','Proposition 3.8.2: higher cohomology classes vanish locally','For q positive, every local cohomology class becomes zero on a sufficiently small neighborhood of a given point.','Represent the class by a cocycle in an injective resolution and use exactness of that resolution on stalks to find a local primitive; restriction preserves injective resolutions. The positive-degree condition is essential and is corrected in the text.','local vanishing|cohomology presheaf|positive degree')
add('P176-0002','paracompact refinement theorem cited','§3.8: global Cech-to-derived comparison','On the stated paracompact spaces, Cech cohomology agrees with derived sheaf cohomology through the canonical map.','Prove that the locally zero positive cohomology presheaves have zero Cech cohomology after passing through suitably fine locally finite refinements. This requires simultaneous refinement over intersections, not just the pointwise vanishing of individual germs. Then the global cover spectral sequence collapses.','paracompact|Cech derived comparison|refinement')
